<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ayacucho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> was fought on 9 December 1824 in the Peruvian highlands, where a patriot army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> defeated and captured the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-la-serna">José de la Serna</a>. The capitulation that followed ended the royalist army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and effectively ended Spanish rule in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, opening the way to the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho.jpg" alt="Battle of Ayacucho" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martín Tovar y Tovar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Ayacucho_by_Mart%C3%ADn_Tovar_y_Tovar_(1827_-_1902).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
